<\body>
  <section*|Exercise: Data Visualization – California Housing Dataset Analysis><label|exercise-data-visualization-california-housing-dataset-analysis>

  <with|font-series|bold|Name:Haorong Guo \ (郭昊荣) \ \ Student ID: 12411217>\ 

  <hrule>

  <subsection*|1. Task 1: Feature Distribution Visualization with Kernel Density Estimation><label|task-1-feature-distribution-visualization-with-kernel-density-estimation>

  <subsubsection*|<with|font-series|bold|1.1 KDE of features>>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item> KDE With Original Datas(set kernel width by Scott's Rule)
  </itemize-dot>

  <image|<tuple|<#>|KDE_Distribution.png>|0.8par|||>

  <\padded-center>
    Figure 1
  </padded-center>

  \;

  <\strong>
    <\itemize-dot>
      <item>KDE with Transformed Data
    </itemize-dot>
  </strong>

  we can find that in Figure 1 ,AveRooms, AveBedrms, Population, AveOccup are obviously right-skewed features ,and their "tail" are too long.Therefore, in order to make the data more intuitive, we have filtered out all data points of those features that exceed the 99th percentile,and then transformed those features via the log1p function: <math|>

  <\equation*>
    y=ln <around|(|x+1|)>
  </equation*>

  where y denotes the transformed feature and x represents the original raw data.

  and we get KDE with Transformed Data,figurre 2.

  \;

  <with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|All Continuous Features.png>|0.8par|||>>

  <\padded-center>
    Figure 2
  </padded-center>

  <paragraph|<with|font-series|bold|<paragraph*|1.2>Method And parameters to set Kernel width >>

  <subparagraph|1.2.1>The final Kernel bandwidth\ 

  The final Kernel bandwidth is set using Scott's Rule without robust correction

  <\equation*>
    h=1.059\<times\>\<sigma\>\<times\>n<rsup|-1/5>
  </equation*>

  \;

  h: optimal kernel bandwidth

  1.059: constant for Gaussian kernel

  \<sigma\>: standard deviation of the sample

  n: sample size

  <subparagraph|1.2.2>process and reasons of using Scott's Rule

  When determining the kernel width, I first analyzed the relationship between the kernel width and my research goal. Since I aimed to observe clear trends in the data from the visualization—such as the type of distribution and the number of peaks—I needed a relatively smooth curve, which suggests a smaller kernel width.

  \;

  I also reviewed commonly considered parameters and their roles for this dataset, including the sample size (to avoid noise while preserving real trends), the type of data distribution (using a smaller kernel width to sharpen peaks and improve interpretability), and the standard deviation.

  \;

  After manually testing several values with unsatisfactory results, I realized that balancing these parameters to choose an appropriate kernel width is non\<nbhyph\>trivial. I then investigated standard, widely used methods for kernel bandwidth selection and finally adopted <strong|Scott's method>, which is both common and effective.

  \;

  I implemented a simplified version of Scott's formula through programming, rather than using the built\<nbhyph\>in function from Python libraries, because the library implementation is more complex, encapsulated, and does not provide direct access to the exact bandwidth value.

  \;

  The outcome matched my expectations: the resulting curve was smooth, retained important structural features, and clearly showed significant peaks. Therefore, I used this method and its corresponding parameters to determine the kernel width. All the exact values of bandwidth are remarked in figures.\ 

  <subsubsection*|1.3 \ Discussion Of Distribution And Interesting Phenomenon >

  <with|font-series|bold| \ \ \ 1. AveRooms (Average Rooms per Household)>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Distribution>: Unimodal, strongly right-skewed, with a sharp peak at <textasciitilde>1.85(transformated).

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Interesting finding>: The log1p transformation compresses the right tail, revealing a tight concentration of households with <textasciitilde>6–7 rooms (before log transformation), while a small secondary bump suggests a tiny fraction of larger households.I think developers tend to build homes with either many or few rooms. Here is my conjecture:Large homes are popular with wealthy buyers, while small ones suit average people, so medium-sized homes are less attractive. But from another perspectivehe, second peak may show a threshold where more rooms greatly increase prices. It may also be caused by the way the data was collected. I have several possible explanations but am unsure which is correct.
  </itemize>

  <with|font-series|bold| \ \ \ \ 2. AveBedrms (Average Bedrooms per Household)>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Distribution>: \ Narrow unimodal peak at <textasciitilde>0.71(transformated), with a very small right tail.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Interesting finding>: The distribution is far more concentrated than <with|font-family|tt|AveRooms>, indicating most households have <textasciitilde>1–2 bedrooms. Still there is a small secondary bump.
  </itemize>

  \;

  \ \ \ \ \ <subsection*|3. <with|font-family|tt|Population> (Block Group Population)><label|population_log1p-block-group-population-log1p-transformed>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Distribution>: Unimodal, right-skewed, peaking at <textasciitilde>7.07.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Interesting finding>: The log transformation reveals a clear central tendency, with population concentrated around <textasciitilde>1100–1200 people per block group. Still there is a small secondary bump.
  </itemize>

  \;

  <subsection*|4. <with|font-family|tt|AveOccup> (Average Household Occupancy)><label|aveoccup_log1p-average-household-occupancy-log1p-transformed>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Distribution>: Unimodal, slightly right-skewed, peak at <textasciitilde>1.33(transformated).

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Interesting finding>: The peak corresponds to an average household size of <textasciitilde>2.8 people, aligning with typical U.S. household sizes. Still there is a small secondary bump.
  </itemize>

  \;

  <subsection*|5. <with|font-family|tt|HouseAge> (Median House Age)><label|houseage-median-house-age>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Distribution>: Multi-modal, with three distinct peaks at <textasciitilde>16.84, <textasciitilde>25.85, and <textasciitilde>34.88, plus a smaller fourth peak near 50.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Interesting finding>: This reveals clear cohorts of housing stock:

    <\itemize>
      <item>Early 20th-century homes (peak <textasciitilde>17)

      <item>Mid-century homes (peak <textasciitilde>26)

      <item>Late 20th-century homes (peak <textasciitilde>35)

      <item>A small group of very new homes (near 0) and very old homes (near 50)
    </itemize>
  </itemize>

  This multi-modal structure directly reflects historical building booms in California.\ 

  <subsection*|6. <with|font-family|tt|Latitude>><label|latitude>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Distribution>: Bimodal, with a dominant peak at <textasciitilde>33.99 and a secondary peak at <textasciitilde>37.73.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Interesting finding>: The two peaks correspond to major population centers in Southern California (e.g., Los Angeles/Orange County, <textasciitilde>34\<degree\>N) and Northern California (e.g., Bay Area, <textasciitilde>38\<degree\>N), clearly showing the state's two largest metropolitan clusters.I got the exact placenames by asking AI.
  </itemize>

  \;

  <subsection*|7. <with|font-family|tt|Longitude>><label|longitude>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Distribution>: Bimodal, with peaks at <textasciitilde>-122.14 and <textasciitilde>-118.18.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Interesting finding>: These peaks align with the Bay Area (<textasciitilde>-122\<degree\>W) and Southern California coastal regions (<textasciitilde>-118\<degree\>W), mirroring the latitude bimodality and confirming the coastal concentration of California's population.I got the exact placenames and imfomations by asking AI.
  </itemize>

  \;

  <subsection*|8. <with|font-family|tt|MedInc> (Median Income)><label|medinc-median-income>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Distribution>: Unimodal, right-skewed, peak at <textasciitilde>3.11 (i.e., $311,000 median income per block group).

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Interesting finding>: I think the distribution is consistent with common sense.
  </itemize>

  \;

  \;

  \;

  \;

  <subsection*|2. Task 2: Bimodal Quantitative Features><label|task-2-bimodal-quantitative-features>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|Latitude>

    <item><strong|Longitude>
  </enumerate>

  (I got the exact placenames and imfomations about the Latitude and Longitude at peaks by asking AI.)

  <subsubsection*|1. Latitude (peaks at ~32.86, ~33.99, ~37.73)>

  <\itemize>
    <item>The two dominant peaks correspond to California's two major population clusters:

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Southern California (~34\<degree\>N)>: Includes Los Angeles, Orange County, and San Diego, the state's most populous region.

      <item><strong|Northern California (~38\<degree\>N)>: Includes the San Francisco Bay Area (San Francisco, Oakland, San Jose), another major economic and population center.
    </itemize>

    <item>The smaller peak at ~32.86 likely reflects the San Diego metropolitan area, a distinct southern coastal cluster.
  </itemize>

  <subsubsection*|2. Longitude (peaks at ~-122.14, ~-118.18)>

  <\itemize>
    <item>The peaks align with California's coastal population centers:

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Bay Area (~-122\<degree\>W)>: Centered on San Francisco and the surrounding Bay Area, a key tech and urban hub on the central coast.

      <item><strong|Southern California (~-118\<degree\>W)>: Centered on Los Angeles and Orange County, the state's largest urban area on the southern coast.
    </itemize>

    <item>This bimodality directly reflects California's population being concentrated along the Pacific coast in two distinct metropolitan regions, rather than being evenly distributed inland.
  </itemize>

  \;

  <subsection*|3. Task 3: Features Where the Average Is Not Representative>

  ”\ 

  <subsubsection*|3.1 List of Non-Representative Average Features><label|list-of-non-representative-mean-features>

  <\itemize>
    <label|task-3-features-where-the-mean-is-not-representative>AveRooms,AveBedrms, Population, AveOccup,Latitude,Longitude,MedInc,HouseAge
  </itemize>

  <subsubsection*|3.2 Explanation for Each Feature><label|explanation-for-each-feature>

  All of the right-skewed features's(AveRooms,AveBedrms, Population, AveOccup,HouseAge) average isn't renprenstative.Because the average is pulled upward by the long right tail of large values, while the majority of data points cluster around the left (lower values) near the median/peak.

  And In a <strong|<with|font-series||bimodal distribution(Latitude,Longitude)><with|font-series||>>, the data has two distinct and separate peaks, so yhe mean lies in the low density space between the two clusters, representing nothing characteristic of the data.

  In a multimodal distribution(HouseAge),I think it is hard to say if it is representative. But as far as I'm concernd, average seems lie <strong|between the peaks(even close to)>,It cannot represent any of the main groups, so I think it is not representative.

  <subsection*|4. Task 4: Most Predictive Features for House Value><label|task-4-most-predictive-features-for-house-value>

  <subsubsection*|4.1 Selection of Top 3 Predictive Features><label|selection-of-top-3-predictive-features>

  <\itemize>
    <item> <with|font-family|tt|median_income>, <with|font-family|tt|latitude>, <with|font-family|tt|longitude>,\ 
  </itemize>

  <subsubsection*|4.2 Reasons><label|justification>

  For each selected feature:\ 

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|font-family|tt|median_income>: I think it is a strong direct correlation with house value .Higher income areas can afford more expensive housing,and expensive houses are always built together to attract rich people.So I think median_income will be predicitive.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|<with|font-family|tt|latitude> /<with|font-family|tt|longitude> >: Location is often one of the most important indicators of housing prices. In previous tasks, we found a correlation between latitude and longitude and the popularity of actual locations, and I believe this also has a very strong correlation with housing prices.

    \;

    \;
  </itemize>

  \;

  \;

  \;
